var e=1,t=`eth-conflicts-01`,n=`ethics`,r=`Conflicts of Interest: Identify, Then Decide What to Do About It`,i=`A conflict is a structural fact rather than a moral failing — and the useful question is whether it is being avoided, controlled, or merely written down.`,a=`core`,o=[`eth-duties-01`],s=9,c=[`CFA-L1`,`CAIA-L1`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`A conflict of interest exists when someone's own interest could reasonably influence a judgement they make on another's behalf. **It does not require anyone to act on it.** The conflict is the situation, not the behaviour.

That distinction matters because it removes the defensiveness. Saying "there is a conflict here" is a description, not an allegation, and people who understand that are much easier to work with on the subject.

Three responses exist, in descending order of strength:

**Avoid.** Do not take the position, do not accept the arrangement, recuse from the decision. The only response that eliminates the conflict.

**Control.** Keep the conflict and constrain the discretion — an independent decision-maker, a pre-agreed formula, an external price reference, a consent requirement.

**Disclose.** Tell the affected party. Necessary in every case and sufficient in fewer than people assume.

**The failure is usually not choosing badly among the three. It is treating disclosure as the whole answer** — documenting a conflict in a paragraph nobody can act on, and considering the matter closed.`},{type:`concept`,id:`structural-conflicts`,body:`**In alternatives the significant conflicts are structural and permanent, so they cannot be resolved once and forgotten.**

**Self-valuation.** The manager marks its own portfolio, and those marks drive reported performance, fundraising and accrued carry.

**Cross-fund transactions.** Selling an asset from one of the manager's funds to another. The manager sets the price and represents both sides.

**Continuation vehicles.** Moving an asset into a new vehicle with new investors, advising both sides and earning fees on both.

**Allocation of scarce opportunities.** Which fund gets the deal, and which investors get co-investment. Commercially valuable investors are an obvious temptation.

**Fee and expense allocation.** Which costs sit with the manager and which with the fund.

**Multiple funds with different economics.** A manager running funds at different fee levels or different points in their carry has an interest in where a deal lands.

**Successor fund fundraising.** The period before a new fund launch creates pressure on marks, on realisation timing and on how performance is presented.

**None of these can be avoided while running the business.** So every one has to be controlled, and the quality of the control is the entire question. **"Disclosed in the partnership agreement" is the weakest possible answer to any of them.**`},{type:`intuition`,id:`the-three-questions`,body:`**Three questions establish whether a conflict is genuinely managed, and they work on any conflict in any context.**

**Who decides, and what do they gain from the outcome?** If the decision-maker's compensation depends on the answer, there is no control — only a disclosure. An independent voice means someone whose pay does not move with the decision.

**Is there an external reference?** A market price, a third-party valuation, a competitive process, a pre-agreed formula. Anything that comes from outside the interested party converts a judgement into a comparison. A cross-fund sale with a market-tested price is a different transaction from one priced internally.

**Can the affected party actually act?** A conflict disclosed before an investor commits, where they can decline or negotiate, has been managed. The same conflict disclosed after the decision, in a document that cannot be changed, has been recorded.

**Applied to a continuation vehicle, these three cut straight to the substance:** was the price market-tested, did an independent body consent, and could investors genuinely choose to exit at that price with adequate information?

**If the answers are yes, yes and yes, the conflict was managed** — and the transaction may be entirely in everyone's interest. If any answer is no, the conflict was documented, and the outcome depended on the good faith of an interested party. **Which may have been perfectly sound and is not a control.**`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-coinvest`,body:`A manager has a scarce co-investment opportunity of 50. Investor A has 100 committed and is considering 200 in the next fund. Investor B has 100 committed and has said it will not re-up. Pro rata, each would receive 25.`,walkthrough:[`**The commercial temptation is obvious.** Allocating more to Investor A supports a 200 commitment; Investor B offers nothing further.`,`**Test one — who decides and what do they gain?** If the decision sits with the person responsible for fundraising, the conflict is unmanaged by construction.`,`**Test two — is there an external reference?** A written allocation policy agreed in advance is the reference here. Pro rata by commitment is the simplest, and any other rule is acceptable if it was set before the opportunity arose.`,`**Test three — can the affected party act?** Investor B can only object if it knows the allocation was discretionary and what the policy is.`,`**Read that carefully: the ethical content is entirely in whether the rule existed before the opportunity.** A pro rata policy written last year is a control. The same allocation decided this week is a judgement that happened to favour the commercially valuable investor.`,`And note the honest complication: **allocation policies legitimately do favour larger or longer-standing investors**, and that is a commercial term investors can accept in advance. What cannot be defended is deciding case by case in the manager's interest.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **for any discretionary allocation, ask when the policy was written.** Before the opportunity is a control; after is a rationalisation.`]},{type:`table`,id:`conflict-table`,caption:`Structural conflicts and what a real control looks like`,headers:[`Conflict`,`Weak response`,`Real control`],rows:[[`Self-valuation`,`Disclosed in the offering documents`,`Independent committee members, third-party valuations, realisations tracked against marks.`],[`Cross-fund sale`,`Both funds' documents permit it`,`Market-tested price plus independent consent from both sides.`],[`Continuation vehicle`,`Investors were offered a choice`,`External price discovery, advisory committee consent, adequate information to choose.`],[`[[co-investment|Co-investment]] allocation`,`Allocated at the manager's discretion`,`A written policy agreed before the opportunity arose.`],[`Expense allocation`,`A broad permission in the agreement`,`A specific policy, consistently applied, separately reported.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Disclosure treated as resolution.** The most common failure by a wide margin. A conflict in a document nobody can negotiate has been recorded rather than managed.

**Independence in name only.** A committee whose members are all compensated on fund performance has no independent voice, whatever the charter says.

**Policies written after the fact.** A rule created once the opportunity is known will accommodate the preferred answer.

**Conflicts assessed one at a time.** Individually each may be small. Aggregated, a pattern where every close call favours the manager is the thing an investor eventually notices.

**Personal conflicts overlooked.** Gifts, hospitality, personal investments, a family relationship with a counterparty, a job offer from a company under diligence. Smaller amounts and closer to home, and the disclosure obligations are usually clear and routinely forgotten.

**Assuming a conflict disappears if the outcome was good.** A well-managed conflict and a lucky outcome are different things, and only one of them repeats.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`your-own-conflicts`,body:`**The conflicts most likely to affect a junior analyst are personal, small, and unaddressed because they seem too minor to mention.**

**Hospitality from counterparties.** One dinner is not a problem. Twelve dinners a year from the same broker or placement agent is around 3,000 of undisclosed benefit from a party whose services you help evaluate. **The threshold that matters is not the individual amount; it is the pattern and whether it is disclosed.**

**Personal investments.** Holding a security you may recommend, or investing alongside a manager you diligence, creates a conflict regardless of size. Most firms require pre-clearance, and the reason is not suspicion — it is that the disclosure protects you as much as the client.

**Information you happen to acquire.** A friend at a portfolio company, a family member at a counterparty. Innocent origin does not change the obligation.

**A job offer.** From a manager under diligence, a portfolio company, or a counterparty. This is a live conflict during the diligence and it should be disclosed immediately.

**Relationships.** A close personal connection with someone at a counterparty is disclosable in most policies, and the awkwardness of raising it is exactly why people do not.

**The general rule that covers all of them: if you would rather your supervisor did not know, that is the thing to tell them.** It is uncomfortable once, and the alternative is a conversation about why you did not.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Apply the three questions to any conflict:** who decides and what do they gain, is there an external reference, and can the affected party act?

**For any discretionary allocation, ask when the policy was written.** Before the opportunity is a control; afterwards is a rationalisation.

**Ask who on a valuation or conflicts committee is not compensated on fund performance.** If nobody, the independence is nominal.

**For a continuation vehicle, ask whether the price was market-tested and whether the advisory committee consented.** Both are yes-or-no questions with documentary answers.

**Disclose your own small conflicts early and in writing.** Hospitality patterns, personal holdings, relationships, job conversations. The disclosure costs one uncomfortable conversation and removes the whole risk.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`A conflict is a situation, not a behaviour — which means naming one is a description rather than an accusation.`,`Three responses: avoid, control, disclose. Disclosure is always necessary and much less often sufficient than it is treated as being.`,`Three questions test any conflict: who decides and what do they gain, is there an external reference, and can the affected party act?`,`The ethical content of a discretionary allocation is whether the policy existed before the opportunity. Twelve dinners a year from one counterparty is 3,000 of undisclosed benefit — the pattern is the issue, not the amount.`]}],u=[{id:`eth-conflicts-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`conflicts`,`definition`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`When does a conflict of interest exist?`,choices:[`Only when someone actually acts against a client's interest`,`When someone's own interest could reasonably influence a judgement they make on another's behalf — regardless of whether they act on it`,`Only when it is undisclosed`,`When two clients want the same investment`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`That is acting on a conflict. The conflict itself is the situation that precedes any action.`,`Correct. The conflict is structural, which is why identifying one is a neutral description rather than an allegation about anyone's conduct.`,`Disclosure changes how a conflict is handled, not whether it exists.`,`That is one specific case; conflicts arise far more broadly than competing client demands.`],explanation:`Getting this right makes the subject workable in practice. "There's a conflict here" is a statement people can engage with; "you have a conflict of interest" as an accusation produces defensiveness and no information.`},{id:`eth-conflicts-01-q2`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`conflicts`,`responses`],concept:`the-three-questions`,stem:`A manager proposes selling an asset from one of its funds to another fund it manages. Which response constitutes a real control?`,choices:[`Disclosing the transaction in both funds' reporting`,`A market-tested price plus independent consent from advisory committees on both sides`,`Obtaining an internal valuation from the firm's finance team`,`Confirming that both funds' documents permit the transaction`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Disclosure after the fact records the transaction; it does not constrain the price or the decision.`,`Correct. An external price reference removes the manager's discretion over the number, and independent consent removes its unilateral authority over the decision.`,`An internal valuation is still produced by the interested party.`,`Permission is a defence rather than a control — it establishes that the transaction is allowed, not that it was fair.`],explanation:`The pattern generalises: a real control either removes the discretion or removes the decision from the conflicted party. Anything that leaves both in place and adds documentation is disclosure wearing a control's clothing.`},{id:`eth-conflicts-01-q3`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`conflicts`,`allocation`],concept:`worked-coinvest`,stem:`A scarce co-investment is allocated in a way that favours an investor about to commit to the next fund. What determines whether this is defensible?`,choices:[`Whether the favoured investor performs well on the co-investment`,`Whether the allocation policy was written and agreed before the opportunity arose`,`Whether the other investors complain`,`Whether the amounts involved are material`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Outcomes do not validate a process, and a good outcome from a discretionary decision is still discretionary.`,`Correct. A policy that legitimately favours larger or longer-standing investors is a commercial term accepted in advance. The same decision made once the opportunity is known is a judgement in the manager's interest.`,`Absence of complaint reflects information asymmetry rather than acceptance.`,`Materiality affects the size of the problem, not whether the process was sound.`],explanation:`"When was the policy written?" is a remarkably efficient question. It applies to allocation, expense treatment, valuation methodology and fee arrangements, and the answer is documentary rather than a matter of opinion.`},{id:`eth-conflicts-01-q4`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`conflicts`,`misconception`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`"Every conflict in our funds is disclosed in the partnership agreement, so they are managed." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — disclosure in a document investors cannot negotiate, about decisions not yet taken, records a conflict rather than controlling it`,`False — partnership agreements cannot disclose conflicts`,`True — full disclosure is the standard for managing conflicts`,`False — because conflicts must always be avoided entirely`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. A control removes the discretion or removes the decision from the interested party. Broad disclosure in a non-negotiable document does neither, though it is a necessary first step.`,`They routinely do disclose them, and that is the practice under examination.`,`Disclosure is the minimum rather than the standard, and for structural conflicts it is rarely sufficient.`,`Avoidance is impossible for the structural conflicts inherent in running funds — hence controls.`],explanation:`The productive follow-up is specific: for each structural conflict, what is the control? Self-valuation, cross-fund sales and co-investment allocation each have well-established controls, and a manager who has implemented them will describe them readily.`},{id:`eth-conflicts-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`conflicts`,`personal`,`on-the-job`],concept:`your-own-conflicts`,stem:`You receive a job offer from a manager your team is currently conducting diligence on. What should you do?`,choices:[`Nothing, provided you decline the offer`,`Disclose it immediately and recuse yourself from the diligence`,`Wait until the diligence concludes, then disclose`,`Disclose it only if you intend to accept`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Declining removes your future interest and not the fact that your judgement was exposed to an inducement during the process.`,`Correct. It is a live conflict for as long as the offer exists, and recusal is the only response that removes it from the decision.`,`Waiting means the conflicted judgement has already been recorded in the work.`,`The conflict exists on receipt of the offer, regardless of your intention.`],explanation:`This is one of the cleanest cases because avoidance is genuinely available: step out of the process. It also protects your own position — a recommendation made while holding an offer from the subject is impossible to defend afterwards, whatever its merits.`},{id:`eth-conflicts-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`conflicts`,`hospitality`,`on-the-job`],concept:`your-own-conflicts`,stem:`You have accepted twelve dinners over a year from one placement agent whose funds you evaluate. Why does this matter?`,choices:[`It does not — each dinner was modest`,`The pattern amounts to roughly 3,000 of undisclosed benefit from a party whose services you assess, and the pattern rather than the individual amount is the issue`,`It matters only if the agent's funds were selected`,`It matters only if the firm's policy sets a lower threshold`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Each dinner was modest and the accumulation is not, which is why per-event thresholds miss this case.`,`Correct. Twelve at 250 is around 3,000 from a single interested party. Reciprocity does not require any dinner to be extravagant.`,`The obligation arises from the relationship, not from the outcome.`,`Policy thresholds matter and the underlying obligation exists independently of where a threshold sits.`],explanation:`Most gifts and hospitality policies are written per event, which is exactly why the cumulative case slips through. Recording what you accept and from whom takes minutes a year and answers the question before anyone asks it.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
